  "worked_solution": "For a type III region $R$ with projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane, the triple integral of a continuous function $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\u222d}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\u222c}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe consider the region $R$ as a type III region enclosed between the back surface $y={u}_{1}(x,z)=0$ and the front surface $y={u}_{2}(x,z)=2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070114/assets/63cffdee00edd80c4a0abdc30e8e45b1af29078b8bc4902bf3f60b7b4a104172.png)\n\nSo, the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II *plane* region in the $xz$-plane with the right curve\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070114/assets/dc72aeb4b554d88f00083dc6bf6f8ca4244e1b7683c8dec10ba0491c678a5603.png)\n\nHence, $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le e,\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}\u2061z}.$\n\nTherefore, we write the triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222d}_{R}\\frac{\\pi x}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\frac{\\pi y}{4}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\u222c}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\pi x}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\frac{\\pi y}{4}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z}x[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\frac{\\pi y}{4}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z}x{[\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\frac{\\pi y}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$",
